---
import PhotoPlaceholder from '../PhotoPlaceholder.astro';
import VisionFigure from '../VisionFigure.astro';
import SectionHead from '../SectionHead.astro';
---

<section class="band-cream section rule-thick">
  <SectionHead num="1 / 14 — How we get around" heading="Walk to milk." />

  <div class="container-page">
    <PhotoPlaceholder
      description="A Vegas residential block in the won state — apartments above a corner store, mature trees, a neighbor walking a grocery bag home. Mid-day, light shade."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        There's a way to think about a neighborhood that has nothing to do with property values:
        <em>how far is the milk?</em> Across most of the Las&nbsp;Vegas Valley today, the answer is a
        quarter-mile minimum, usually more, almost always far enough that a car is the only practical
        answer. That isn't a fact about the desert. It's a fact about how we built our streets, how we
        drew the lines that decide what's allowed where, and which mode of transportation we treated as
        the default for every kind of trip.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container-page">
    <VisionFigure num="1" title="What's within a 5-minute walk">
      <svg
        class="walk-radius"
        viewBox="0 0 600 360"
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Diagram of destinations within a 5-minute walk of home: grocery, park, school, BRT stop, café, and pharmacy"
      >
        <circle class="radius-circle" cx="300" cy="180" r="150"></circle>
        <text class="ring-label" x="300" y="40" text-anchor="middle"
          >5-minute walk · ¼ mile radius</text
        >
        <circle class="home-dot" cx="300" cy="180" r="9"></circle>
        <text class="home-label" x="300" y="205" text-anchor="middle">HOME</text>
        <circle class="dest-dot" cx="200" cy="120" r="5"></circle><text
          class="label"
          x="200"
          y="105"
          text-anchor="middle">Grocery</text
        >
        <circle class="dest-dot" cx="395" cy="115" r="5"></circle><text
          class="label"
          x="395"
          y="100"
          text-anchor="middle">Park</text
        >
        <circle class="dest-dot" cx="170" cy="220" r="5"></circle><text
          class="label"
          x="170"
          y="245"
          text-anchor="middle">Elementary school</text
        >
        <circle class="dest-dot" cx="420" cy="220" r="5"></circle><text
          class="label"
          x="420"
          y="245"
          text-anchor="middle">BRT stop</text
        >
        <circle class="dest-dot" cx="300" cy="80" r="5"></circle><text
          class="label"
          x="300"
          y="68"
          text-anchor="middle">Café</text
        >
        <circle class="dest-dot" cx="300" cy="290" r="5"></circle><text
          class="label"
          x="300"
          y="310"
          text-anchor="middle">Pharmacy / clinic</text
        >
      </svg>
      <Fragment slot="caption">
        Most American neighborhoods built before 1950 fit roughly this pattern. Most of the
        Las&nbsp;Vegas Valley does not — by zoning, not by accident.
      </Fragment>
    </VisionFigure>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        The Valley we're building puts the milk five minutes away on foot. It puts a school six. A
        park three. A bus stop ten. The grocery store sits at the corner with an apartment over it;
        the duplex is half a block down. None of this requires bulldozers. Most of it just requires
        changes to a zoning code that has barely been updated since 1965 — the same kind of changes
        a growing list of American cities have already passed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Walking is the cheapest mode of transportation ever invented and the only one that scales to
        every age and every income on the same terms. It's how the world organized daily life for
        most of human history, and how most of the world's most-loved neighborhoods still work
        today. The Valley can have it back. Most of the work is undoing what we did to ourselves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .walk-radius {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 360px;
  }
  .walk-radius .radius-circle {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 6%, transparent);
    stroke: var(--color-accent);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 4;
  }
  .walk-radius .home-dot {
    fill: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .walk-radius .dest-dot {
    fill: var(--vp-text);
  }
  .walk-radius .label {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: currentColor;
  }
  .walk-radius .home-label {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 900;
    fill: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .walk-radius .ring-label {
    font-size: 9px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    fill: var(--color-accent);
  }
</style>
